# POST /calendar/provision — step 1: the body is JSON {"username": …,
# "password"?: …}.
#
# UNAUTHENTICATED BY DESIGN — a loopback demo. Anyone who can reach the
# web port can create or reset an account under a domain this tenant
# owns. The boundary that always holds is in the op, not this route:
# txco://calendar/account runs only inside this tenant's rules and only
# for usernames under a domain the tenant has verified (or serves DNS
# for). WHO may call it is this WHEN clause; a product gates it on its
# own session or provisions from an internal flow (an owner's claim step).
#
# `username` must be <local>@<domain> where <domain> is a hostname this
# tenant owns. Under `txco dev` the dev-local names (localhost,
# *.localhost, *.local.thanks.computer) are auto-verified on bind, so
# `paris@pony.local.thanks.computer` works once that hostname is bound to
# this stack.
WHEN @src == "http" && @web.req.method == "POST" && @web.req.url.path == "/calendar/provision"
  EMIT ._prov = &try_json(&b64decode(@web.req.body)),
       ._prov_req = true
